# The post-reload coherence case, run by the driver AFTER prober_signal_wait
# has confirmed a new worker is answering AND the stalled connection under
# test has been force-closed. It proves the server came back HEALTHY: the
# reloaded worker serves a correct 200, leaks no descriptor, and logs nothing
# on the error path in the window around THIS request.
#
# The pid oracle is STRICT (no pid_may_change), and that is correct for the
# same reason as reload-mid-upload/post-reload.rule: by the time this file
# runs, a single post-reload worker is serving, so both of the case's probe
# snapshots see the SAME worker -- "same worker pid" is the stronger, true
# assertion. The reload-survival / force-close claims this scenario exists to
# make are the driver's own numbered assertions (the stalled conn stays open
# until worker_shutdown_timeout, then is force-closed), not this leg.
#
# no_error_log here is scoped to \[error\] on THIS case's own before/after
# log window (prober_scrape_log/no_error_log only look at log growth around
# this one case). The worker_shutdown_timeout force-close emits NO
# [error]/[warn]/[alert] line of its own (empirically verified -- it is a
# plain graceful-exit fd close), so there is nothing for this leg to trip on
# either way; the window scoping is belt-and-suspenders.

# WHY THE fd ORACLE IS SPLIT BY KIND. This scenario's whole point is that the
# draining worker FORCE-CLOSES the stalled connection when
# worker_shutdown_timeout expires. That close is asynchronous with respect to
# this rule, so it can land inside the probe window and drop the count by one --
# `delta fds == 0` then reds on the very behaviour the scenario exists to prove.
# It is the same class as backend-idle-close-reload (see that scenario's
# post-reload.rule for the long form) with a different descriptor: there it is
# the old worker's parked UPSTREAM socket, here it is the force-closed CLIENT
# socket. Either way only the socket bucket can legitimately fall, so only it is
# relaxed, and only downwards -- an INCREASE still reds, which is the shape a
# leak takes. file/anon/other keep the original strict `== 0`.
#
# Filed as a flake since s126 and misdiagnosed then as a readiness wait being
# too tight. It is not a timing-tolerance problem: CI run 30356435513
# (`scenarios nginx 1.29.0 san`) reds with `delta fds: 13 -> 12 is -1`, the
# byte-identical signature backend-idle-close-reload showed, on a diff that
# does not touch this scenario.
#
# `delta` is `after - before`, so this rules out an UNOFFSET net socket
# increase; a leak exactly coinciding with the force-close nets to zero and
# passes. That blind spot is not introduced here -- `delta fds == 0` had it too.

name    the reloaded worker serves cleanly after the stalled-upload force-close
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
delta   fds_by_kind.socket <= 0
delta   fds_by_kind.file == 0
delta   fds_by_kind.anon == 0
delta   fds_by_kind.other == 0
no_error_log  \[error\]
